 ## Overview
-Voxel Splat Rendering is an object-order volume rendering technique where each voxel is projected onto the screen as a 2D footprint (splat) and composited into the image buffer [S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering large volumetric datasets interactively by replacing per-pixel ray traversal with per-voxel projection, allowing for efficient culling of irrelevant voxels and parallelization [S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Circle Splat Rendering](2981-circle-splat-rendering.md) which uses circular footprints, or [Tetrahedral Splatting](3181-tetrahedral-splatting.md) which preserves volume continuity, Voxel Splat Rendering typically uses square or circular splats derived from the voxel's axis-aligned bounding box or a Gaussian kernel, often resulting in blocky artifacts at close range but offering high performance for distant views [S1][S3, unofficial].
+Voxel Splat Rendering is an object-order volume rendering technique where each voxel is projected onto the screen as a 2D footprint (splat) and composited into the image buffer [S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering large volumetric datasets interactively by replacing per-pixel ray traversal with per-voxel projection, allowing for efficient culling of irrelevant voxels and parallelization [S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Circle Splat Rendering](2981-circle-splat-rendering.md) which uses circular footprints, Voxel Splat Rendering typically projects axis-aligned bounding rectangles or squares, often optimized for GPU rasterization of quads [S1]. It differs from [Image-Space Sheet-Buffered Splatting Pipeline](8961-image-space-sheet-buffered-splatting-pipeline.md) by typically processing voxels in object space (often front-to-back) rather than pre-sorting them into image-space slabs on the GPU [S3, unofficial][S5, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
 *   Volumetric dataset $V$ (array of voxels with position, color, opacity, and optionally normal).
-*   Camera parameters (Model-View-Projection matrix $MVP$).
-*   Rendering parameters: point-size, alpha-value, gamma-value, anti-aliasing [S2, unofficial].
+*   Camera parameters: Model-View-Projection (MVP) matrix or separate Model-View (MV) and Projection (P) matrices.
+*   Rendering parameters: Point size, alpha value, gamma value, and anti-aliasing settings [S2, unofficial].
 
 **Outputs:**
-*   2D Image buffer (color and opacity).
+*   2D Image buffer (Color and Depth/Alpha).
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Voxel:** Contains position $(x,y,z)$, color $(R,G,B)$, opacity $\alpha$, and optionally a normal vector $N$. Normals can be quantized to 240 pre-defined directions stored in 1 byte [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Footprint Table:** A 2D lookup table storing the analytically integrated kernel function along a traversing ray, used to spread energy across multiple pixels [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Voxel:** Contains 3D position $(x,y,z)$, color $(R,G,B)$, opacity $\alpha$, and optionally a surface normal $\mathbf{n}$ [S2, unofficial][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Splat Footprint:** A 2D region in screen space. For axis-aligned splats, this is defined by an axis-aligned bounding rectangle (AABB) in screen coordinates [S1]. For Gaussian splats, it is a radially symmetric kernel [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Image Buffer:** Accumulates color and opacity.
 
 **Steps:**
 
-1.  **Voxel Selection and Culling:**
-    *   Traverse the volume. Optionally, cull voxels based on normal orientation (back-face culling) to reduce processing by up to half [S3, unofficial].
-    *   For interactive selection, retrieve and decode only voxelsets corresponding to selected colors within a bounding box [S2, unofficial].
+1.  **Voxel Transformation:**
+    For each voxel in the volume, transform its center position $\mathbf{p}_{obj}$ from object space to screen space $\mathbf{p}_{screen}$ using the MVP matrix [S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
+    $$ \mathbf{p}_{screen} = M_{MVP} \cdot \mathbf{p}_{obj} $$
+    If the voxel is behind the camera or outside the view frustum, cull it [S3, unofficial].
 
-2.  **Projection to Screen Space:**
-    *   For each voxel, transform its center from object space to screen space using the MVP matrix [S3, unofficial].
-    *   Calculate the axis-aligned bounding rectangle of the projected voxel in screen space [S1].
-    *   *Note:* In parallel projection, the footprint size is constant. In perspective projection, it varies [S4, unofficial].
+2.  **Footprint Calculation:**
+    Determine the screen-space extent of the voxel.
+    *   **Axis-Aligned (Rectangular):** Compute the axis-aligned bounding rectangle of the projected voxel cube. This is often done by projecting the 8 corners of the voxel cube and finding the min/max $u,v$ coordinates [S1].
+    *   **Gaussian (Radial):** Define a radius $r$ based on the voxel size and distance to camera, or use a fixed footprint size for parallel projection [S4, unofficial].
 
-3.  **Weighting (Optional, for Multi-View/IBR):**
-    *   If rendering for novel views from a capture rig, compute a weight $w[i]$ for each camera $i$ to blend contributions [S1]:
-        $$
-        \begin{aligned}
-        dotV &= \text{dot}(VtoEye, VtoCamera[i]) \\
-        dotN &= \text{dot}(Normal, VtoCamera[i]) \\
-        weightV &= \begin{cases} dotV^4 & \text{if } dotV > 0.0 \\ 0.0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} \\
-        weightN &= \begin{cases} dotN^4 & \text{if } dotN > 0.0 \\ 0.0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} \\
-        w[i] &= weightV \times weightN
-        \end{aligned}
-        $$
-        Normalize weights by dividing by $\sum w[i]$. The power exponent is typically 4 [S1].
+3.  **Shading and Weighting (Optional but common):**
+    Calculate a weight for the voxel to modulate its contribution.
+    *   **Normal-based Culling/Weighting:** If normals are available, compute a weight based on the angle between the view vector and the normal to simulate surface shading or cull back-facing voxels [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+    *   **Multi-View Weighting (e.g., for NeRF/Image-based modeling):** If rendering from a novel view using multiple source cameras, compute weights for each source camera $i$ [S1]:
+        1.  Calculate $\text{dotV} = \text{dot}(\mathbf{V}_{toEye}, \mathbf{V}_{toCamera[i]})$.
+        2.  Calculate $\text{dotN} = \text{dot}(\mathbf{n}, \mathbf{V}_{toCamera[i]})$.
+        3.  $\text{weightV} = \text{dotV} > 0.0 ? \text{pow}(\text{dotV}, 4) : 0.0$.
+        4.  $\text{weightN} = \text{dotN} > 0.0 ? \text{pow}(\text{dotN}, 4) : 0.0$.
+        5.  $\text{weight}[i] = \text{weightV} \times \text{weightN}$.
+        6.  Normalize weights by dividing by the sum of all weights [S1].
 
-4.  **Footprint Generation (Splatting):**
-    *   Represent the voxel as a basis function (e.g., Gaussian kernel) scaled by voxel value [S3, unofficial].
-    *   Generate a 2D footprint by rasterizing a precomputed lookup table. Each entry stores the integrated kernel value [S3, unofficial].
-    *   Alternatively, render the bounding rectangle as a quad (pair of triangles) and texture-map the splat, weighting the contribution based on distance from the center [S1].
+4.  **Rasterization (Splatting):**
+    Render the footprint onto the image buffer.
+    *   **Rectangular Splat:** Render the AABB as a pair of triangles (quad) [S1].
+    *   **Gaussian Splat:** Rasterize a precomputed footprint lookup table (LUT) that stores the analytically integrated kernel function along a traversing ray [S3, unofficial]. This spreads the voxel's energy across multiple pixels to counter holes caused by discrete processing [S4, unofficial].
 
 5.  **Compositing:**
-    *   **Front-to-Back:** Traverse voxels from near to far. For each splat, alpha-blend its color and opacity into the image buffer [S4, unofficial][S5, unofficial].
-    *   **Image-Aligned (Mueller):** Process voxels within slabs parallel to the image plane. Clip kernels to the slab, sum into a sheet buffer, and composite the sheet with the previous one [S3, unofficial]. This avoids brightness variations seen in slice-based summation [S3, unofficial].
-    *   Apply alpha-blending and gamma-correction to reduce contributions from tissues with differing density [S2, unofficial].
+    Accumulate the splat's color and opacity into the image buffer.
+    *   **Front-to-Back:** Traverse voxels from near to far. For each splat, update the buffer using alpha blending [S4, unofficial][S5, unofficial]:
+        $$ C_{out} = C_{in} \cdot (1 - \alpha_{splat}) + C_{splat} \cdot \alpha_{splat} $$
+        $$ \alpha_{out} = \alpha_{in} \cdot (1 - \alpha_{splat}) + \alpha_{splat} $$
+    *   **Back-to-Front:** Also possible, but front-to-back is standard for early termination in raycasting, though splatting often processes all visible voxels [S4, unofficial].
+
+6.  **Post-Processing:**
+    Apply gamma correction and alpha blending adjustments to reduce contributions from tissues with differing color densities [S2, unofficial].
 
