+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Holes:** Discrete processing can produce holes on the periphery or during extreme perspective projection. Countered by distributing energy across multiple pixels via a footprint table [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Aliasing:** Minor aliasing artifacts similar to non-anti-aliased triangle models. Close-range views show rectangular block artifacts and pixelation from finite RGB image resolution [S1].
+*   **Blurring:** Tends to blur edges of objects and reduce image contrast [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Requires extensive filtering, making it time-consuming for large datasets if not optimized [S3, unofficial]. GPU rasterization is not always optimized for single-pixel quads, but performance is often acceptable [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Image-Space Sheet-Buffered Splatting Pipeline](8961-image-space-sheet-buffered-splatting-pipeline.md)
-A GPU-accelerated variant that processes voxels in slabs parallel to the image plane, performing bucket construction and depth sorting on the GPU to avoid CPU-GPU bottlenecks. It accelerates rendering by 2-2.5x compared to CPU-based bucketing.
+A GPU-accelerated variant that processes voxels in slabs parallel to the image plane, avoiding CPU-GPU bottlenecks by constructing buckets and sorting on the GPU. It accelerates rendering by 2-2.5x compared to CPU-based bucketing.
 
 ### [Orthographic Voxel Sprite Rendering](16665-orthographic-voxel-sprite-rendering.md)
-Projects voxels using parallel rays to create a flat billboard appearance, solving per-eye pixel shimmer in VR by rendering a single static image identical for both eyes. It uses a single fragment pass with a chunky virtual-pixel grid.
+Uses parallel rays to project voxels onto a 2D sprite plane, solving per-eye pixel shimmer in VR by rendering a single static image identical for both eyes. It uses a single fragment pass with a chunky virtual-pixel grid.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot A)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels and $A$ is the average area (in pixels) covered by a splat [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for voxel storage, plus $O(W \cdot H)$ for the image buffer and footprint tables [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves interactive frame rates (e.g., 1.1 Hz on a 150MHz R4400 for a $256^3$ volume with pre-processing) [S3, unofficial]. GPU implementations are significantly faster, though rasterization of single-pixel quads may not be fully optimized [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient culling of irrelevant voxels; suitable for parallelization via object-space subdivision; allows interactive parameter tuning (alpha, gamma, point-size) [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Tends to blur object edges and reduce contrast [S3, unofficial]. Discrete processing causes holes on periphery or under extreme perspective projection [S4, unofficial]. Close-range views show rectangular block artifacts and pixelation [S1]. Requires extensive filtering, making it time-consuming for high-quality results [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Interactive visualization of large datasets where performance is critical and slight blurring is acceptable.
-*   **When not to use:** High-fidelity medical imaging requiring sharp edges, or close-up views where voxel structure is visible.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot A)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels and $A$ is the average area (in pixels) covered by a splat. Sorting voxels adds $O(N \log N)$ [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing voxel data and $O(W \cdot H)$ for the image buffer [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Achieves 1.1 Hz on a single 150MHz R4400 processor for a $256 \times 256 \times 225$ volume with 65 seconds of pre-processing time [S3, unofficial]. GPU implementations can be significantly faster, with sheet-buffered variants offering 2-2.5x speedup over CPU bucketing [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Interactive frame rates for large datasets; efficient culling of irrelevant voxels; suitable for parallelization based on object space subdivision [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Blurs edges; prone to aliasing and holes if footprint is not properly distributed; brightness variations in animation if not using image-aligned splatting [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Interactive visualization of large volumetric datasets where speed is prioritized over photorealistic edge sharpness [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity edge preservation is critical; when extreme perspective distortion causes severe aliasing [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Voxel Splatting was first introduced by Westover [S3, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. It was proposed as an alternative to ray casting to improve performance by projecting basis functions (typically Gaussian kernels) to the screen and rasterizing precomputed footprints [S3, unofficial]. The method was later refined by Mueller to address brightness variations in animation through image-aligned splatting using slabs [S3, unofficial]. It is widely used in interactive volume visualization systems, particularly for large datasets where out-of-core rendering is necessary [S2, unofficial].
+Voxel Splat Rendering was first published by Westover [S5, unofficial]. It was introduced as an alternative to ray casting, representing the volume as an array of overlapping basis functions (commonly Gaussian kernels) scaled by voxel values [S3, unofficial]. The method was designed to provide interactive frame rates by projecting voxels to the screen and compositing them, rather than casting rays per pixel [S3, unofficial]. Subsequent improvements included image-aligned splatting by Mueller to reduce brightness variations in animation [S3, unofficial], and normal-based culling by Sobierajski et al. to reduce processing load [S3, unofficial]. It is widely used in medical visualization and interactive volume exploration [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
